Absolute pleasure.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.440
Looking forward to this.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:27.360
Okay, so Pepe Escobar, you were one of the last Westerners to see Massoud,

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.600
Ahmad Shah Massoud alive.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:33.360
And so there's nobody better to ask this question.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:39.400
Could you summarize who he was and what's important about him as a historical figure?

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.400
Absolutely.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:49.680
He essentially, he was a great African nationalist hero.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.040
He had a Che Guevara side, let's put it this way.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:02.600
And he also had a ruthless Crypto-Islamist side as well.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.360
So he was a very complex personality.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.440
Born in Afghanistan, he studied in the Lycée Francais in Kabul.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.440
So it was very interesting because he understood French perfectly.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.680
I didn't speak French, he understood French perfectly well.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:26.120
So he was acquainted since he was a kid with French culture at least, if not wider

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.800
European culture.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:30.320
Then he studied engineering.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:38.320
And a twist of fate led him to the anti-Soviet jihad in the 80s.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.840
He was one of the top commanders of the jihad.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:48.320
So these top commanders, they were always passing through or they were connecting in

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:56.840
Peshawar, the capital of the tribal areas with different sorts of alliances, different

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.640
sorts of finance.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:09.520
So Massoud was a very independent, first of all because he was Tajik and most of the other

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.800
commanders were Pashtuns.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:20.480
Russia and China, Russia much later, so I was thinking about Russia and China nowadays.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.120
At the time it was in fact Iran.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:33.760
So the Tajiks in Iran or in Tajikistan, of course, they had a connection with Massoud.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.880
I mentioned Russia because it was fantastic.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:43.560
Later after the jihad and after the fall of Kabul in 95 and the Taliban took power,

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.080
the Russians began a re-approximation.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:52.200
And one of their top interlocutors, their only interlocutor in fact was Massoud

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.880
from 95 to 2001.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.160
With Iran, the same thing.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:05.760
But the big problem started after the end of the jihad, in fact, from 88 to 89 because

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:14.160
then there was a civil war period started in 92 until 95 when the Taliban took power,

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:23.160
where Massoud was directly confronting other big, big characters of the previous jihad,

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.280
especially Gubuddin Haqmatyar.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:33.760
And there was a time when Massoud and Haqmatyar were bombing each other's forces

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.960
in the outskirts and even inside Kabul.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:43.720
And this is absolutely horrendous because if you visited Kabul after

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:54.320
the civil war, during the Taliban reign, 96 to 2001, most of Kabul was destroyed.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:06:00.680
And it was destroyed because of this internecine war between the Hazaras,

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.240
Massoud and Haqmatyar.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.160
So he was not, of course, a saint.

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:13.520
But considering something that I saw for myself and I've never seen

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.960
this anywhere else in the world,

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:23.280
a so-called government straight out of the 7th century Islam, in fact.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.400
This is what Afghanistan was in the late 90s and the early 2000s.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:35.120
There was it was an enormous smuggling and money laundering operation,

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.160
which was financing the Taliban army at the time.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:45.160
And there was no social life except the precept established by this

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:49.160
extremely radical vision of Islam that the Taliban had,

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:54.760
which was from the Dilbandi's, a sect in India from the 19th century.

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:01.560
But with some elements of Wahhabism and Salafi jihadism as well.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:06.960
It was one of the most demented regimes in modern history.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:13.560
One might say, and obviously the only people in different parts of the country

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:18.760
that could profit from it were the people who were linked to their smuggling rings,

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:25.360
especially, for instance, in the border between Afghanistan and Baluchistan,

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:27.160
a place called Springbaldak,

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:32.760
which was probably the largest smuggling operation on earth at the time.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:37.160
And that was one of the largest traffickers.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.960
Exactly, Peter, the largest traffickers.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:44.760
It was fascinating because when I went there the first time, Peter,

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:49.360
I was afraid that I was going to be kidnapped, in fact.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:55.560
And in fact, myself and my photographer, we were appropriated by one of the smugglers.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:00.960
He protected us because he wanted to show us how his business worked.

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:03.960
So that was the way to deal with these people.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:14.160
So Masoud, which will lead us to, I would say, the main point of our conversation,

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:17.760
